    "text": "The solution provided is correct, but let's review it step-by-step to ensure all details are clear and correct.\n\nGiven the function:\n\\[\nf(x) = x(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3)\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the expression by pairing the factors:\n\\[\nf(x) = (x(x + 3))( (x + 1)(x + 2) )\n\\]\n\nNext, expand each pair:\n\\[\nx(x + 3) = x^2 + 3x\n\\]\n\\[\n(x + 1)(x + 2) = x^2 + 3x + 2\n\\]\n\nNow, multiply these results together:\n\\[\nf(x) = (x^2 + 3x)(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\\]\n\nLet \\( y = x^2 + 3x \\). Then the function becomes:\n\\[\nf(x) = y(y + 2) = y^2 + 2y\n\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value of \\( y^2 + 2y \\), we take the derivative and set it to zero:\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dy}(y^2 + 2y) = 2y + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n2y + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n2y = -2\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -1\n\\]\n\nWe check the second derivative to confirm this is a minimum:\n\\[\n\\frac{d^2}{dy^2}(y^2 + 2y) = 2\n\\]\n\nSince the second derivative is positive (\\(2 > 0\\)), the function \\( y^2 + 2y \\) has a local minimum at \\( y = -1 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( y = -1 \\) back into the expression for \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[\nf(x) = y^2 + 2y = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( f(x) = x(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3) \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
